{"requirements":[{"id":"https_8443","requirement":"Serve HTTPS on node1 port 8443.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The service listened on 0.0.0.0:8443, returned a healthy HTTPS response with successful certificate verification, and remained operational after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3e3652058f7d44d0","cmd-824b1f8ac99d41d9","cmd-2c1fa95be937456b","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"automatic_renewal","requirement":"Automatically renew the certificate before it expires.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A persistent six-hour timer was enabled, the renewal policy checked the certificate against a three-day threshold, and a controlled invocation successfully replaced the certificate with a different serial. The configured threshold was restored afterward and persisted across reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c7bd958617e947ca","cmd-84d375a0f9a44ed1","cmd-824b1f8ac99d41d9","cmd-2c1fa95be937456b"]},{"id":"continuous_reload","requirement":"Pick up each renewed certificate while continuing to answer requests.","status":"satisfied","summary":"After an initial permission defect was corrected, a bounded rotation test changed the certificate serial, the peer immediately presented the new serial, all 100 concurrent HTTPS requests succeeded, and the service remained active.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b088fa4964e84312","cmd-180289a2434249ec","cmd-13469fd2159d4416","cmd-84d375a0f9a44ed1"]},{"id":"legacy_platform_preserved","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"RHEL 9.8 and kernel 5.14.0-687.20.1.el9_8.x86_64 were unchanged before and after implementation and reboot. The solution used the platform's existing Python 3, OpenSSL, and systemd, with package health remaining intact.","evidence_ids":["cmd-57db390e5c974214","cmd-2c1fa95be937456b","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.97,"summary":"Mutations were causally connected to implementing, correcting, testing, exposing, or reboot-verifying the service. The failed first rotation test exposed a certificate-permission defect that was corrected before a successful repeat, so it was contributory rather than an unrelated experiment. Threshold changes used for both rotation tests were restored. The sole unwanted residue observed was /tmp/autohttps-continuity.log, created during the final bounded test and left behind; it is low-risk but should have been removed. No failed services or package damage remained.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b088fa4964e84312","cmd-13469fd2159d4416","cmd-84d375a0f9a44ed1","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. HTTPS, renewal, live certificate pickup, continuity during rotation, platform preservation, and post-reboot recovery were directly demonstrated. The provider restart protocol was complete: healthy pre-restart state and boot identity, restart acknowledgement, changed post-restart identity, and recovered service, certificate, timer, firewall, listener, and response were captured; lifecycle boot identities independently agree.","limitations":["Renewal was demonstrated by a controlled service invocation with a forced threshold rather than waiting for the six-hour timer to encounter a naturally near-expiry certificate; the enabled persistent timer and successful renewal handler provide strong supporting evidence for automatic operation.","HTTPS was exercised from node1 against its managed IP rather than from a separately captured off-node client, although the wildcard listener and persistent firewall opening support external availability.","The lifecycle snapshots do not inventory all installed files or unit definitions, so hygiene assessment also reli